  (uuid_generate_v4(), 'appaloosa-turquoise-pear-shaped-stamped-edge-post-earrings', '4578693881', 'https://www.etsy.com/listing/4578693881/appaloosa-turquoise-pear-shaped-stamped?click_key=88861da3-e3d9-479a-bf79-3455fad45126%3ALT68b6e6ce061c658daa8634ff0f485ed6ed4553b3&click_sum=f92a97bb&ls=s&ga_order=most_relevant&ga_search_type=all&ga_view_type=gallery&ga_search_query=Navajo+jewelry&ref=search_grid-729084-8-24&sr_prefetch=1&pf_from=search&frs=1&sts=1&content_source=88861da3-e3d9-479a-bf79-3455fad45126%253ALT68b6e6ce061c658daa8634ff0f485ed6ed4553b3',
   'Appaloosa Turquoise Pear Shaped Stamped Edge Post Earrings', 'These handsome pear-shaped post earrings hold something increasingly difficult to find: first-generation Appaloosa turquoise from the original 2020 discovery. The matched natural cabochons were cut by master lapidary Doug Zachary from that early material, and they display exactly the character that first made Appaloosa such a fascinating new American turquoise. Look closely at these stones. Rather than being predominantly solid blue, little pools and rounded orbs of sky-blue turquoise appear almost like islands scattered across the traditional brown-gray mother rock. One stone is a little more heavily patterned than the other—as natural stones should be—but together they make a remarkably harmonious pair. There is enough similarity to immediately read as a matched set and enough individuality to make each earring interesting in its own right. Appaloosa turquoise was first uncovered in 2020 in Nevada, between Austin and Tonopah, in a remarkable turquoise-producing region that includes such legendary names as Lander Blue, Number Eight, Carico Lake, Fox and Lone Mountain. The earliest Appaloosa material had a particularly distinctive appearance, often showing rounded spots and patches of turquoise against gray and brown host rock. Those markings brought to mind the famously spotted Appaloosa horse and helped give the new turquoise its name. Geneva Ramone, Navajo silversmith, made these earrings in 2026, wisely allowing Doug''s stones to remain the stars. Each cabochon is surrounded by a clean sterling silver border with a deeply stamped outer trim, while three polished silver dew drops crown the top. The design has enough silver presence to frame these unusual stones beautifully without overwhelming them. And Geneva accomplished something important with earrings of this size: she kept the weight down. The pair weighs 13.6 grams total—only 6.8 grams per earring—making them substantial enough to be noticed while remaining light enough for comfortable all-day wear for most', 'turquoise-jewelry',
